prompt_text:
Use the following Problem and Solution to generate a Conversation between a USER and the SYSTEM. Never mention LeetCode. If there are multiple Solution return 2-3 solutions with the hint. If there are multiple different languages in the Solution, the User should ask in a conversation to convert it to the language. NEVER reference the text in Problem or Solution, this is not present in the conversation. It has to be create in the conversation itself. In the first User request use one of the following: - Create a detailled instruction to generate the Code. Describe it fully in detail to be able to generate the Code without any additional information. For complex problems use an input and output example. - Provide the Code and ask for adjustments, clarification or conversion. Never ask for something like that without providing the Code in the User part! Never create conversations where the system just asks for the Code, e.g. 'Absolutely, I can help with that. Please provide me with the C++ code, and I'll convert it to Python for you.'! Make sure the Code is runnable, include all imports and NEVER use placeholders with comments. Additionally create at least 3 rounds of conversation that consists of one of the following but not limited to one of the following: - Examples: Ask for examples how to run the Code and create the inputs and outputs - Optimization: Analyze the code for any potential optimizations in terms of space or time complexity, although the given solution is already optimized with a time complexity of O(N). - Error Handling: Add error handling to manage unexpected inputs or situations, such as empty arrays or invalid target values. - Input Validation: Ensure that the inputs (the array and the target) are valid. For example, you could check that the array contains enough elements to possibly contain a solution. - Code Documentation: Write comprehensive comments and documentation to explain how the code works, which can be helpful for other developers or for future reference. - Refactoring: Refactor the code for better readability and maintainability. This could involve renaming variables to more descriptive names, breaking the code into smaller functions, or applying design patterns where appropriate. - Scalability: Consider how the code would perform with very large input arrays and whether there are any scalability concerns that could be addressed. - User Interface: If this code is part of a larger application, you could create a user interface that allows users to input the array and target value and then displays the result. - Edge Case Testing: Write tests for edge cases, such as arrays with negative numbers, arrays with all zeros, or arrays where multiple pairs sum up to the target (even though the problem states there is exactly one solution). - Benchmarking: Perform benchmark tests to measure the performance of the code under different conditions and with different sizes of input data. - Parallel Processing: If applicable, explore the possibility of parallelizing the code to take advantage of multi-core processors for further performance gains, especially for very large arrays. - Security: If the code is part of a system that handles sensitive data, ensure that it adheres to security best practices to prevent vulnerabilities. - Integration: Consider how this code will integrate with other parts of the system it is intended for, and ensure compatibility and proper interaction with other components. # Problem <p>Some robots are standing on an infinite number line with their initial coordinates given by a <strong>0-indexed</strong> integer array <code>nums</code> and will start moving once given the command to move. The robots will move a unit distance each second.</p> <p>You are given a string <code>s</code> denoting the direction in which robots will move on command. <code>&#39;L&#39;</code> means the robot will move towards the left side or negative side of the number line, whereas <code>&#39;R&#39;</code> means the robot will move towards the right side or positive side of the number line.</p> <p>If two robots collide, they will start moving in opposite directions.</p> <p>Return <em>the sum of distances between all the&nbsp;pairs of robots </em><code>d</code> <em>seconds after&nbsp;the command. </em>Since the sum can be very large, return it modulo <code>10<sup>9</sup> + 7</code>.</p> <p><b>Note: </b></p> <ul> <li>For two robots at the index <code>i</code> and <code>j</code>, pair <code>(i,j)</code> and pair <code>(j,i)</code> are considered the same pair.</li> <li>When robots collide, they <strong>instantly change</strong> their directions without wasting any time.</li> <li>Collision happens&nbsp;when two robots share the same place in a&nbsp;moment. <ul> <li>For example, if a robot is positioned in 0 going to the right and another is positioned in 2 going to the left, the next second they&#39;ll be both in 1 and they will change direction and the next second the first one will be in 0, heading left, and another will be in 2, heading right.</li> <li>For example,&nbsp;if a robot is positioned in 0 going to the right and another is positioned in 1&nbsp;going to the left, the next second the first one will be in 0, heading left, and another will be in 1, heading right.</li> </ul> </li> </ul> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p><strong class="example">Example 1:</strong></p> <pre> <strong>Input:</strong> nums = [-2,0,2], s = &quot;RLL&quot;, d = 3 <strong>Output:</strong> 8 <strong>Explanation:</strong> After 1 second, the positions are [-1,-1,1]. Now, the robot at index 0 will move left, and the robot at index 1 will move right. After 2 seconds, the positions are [-2,0,0]. Now, the robot at index 1 will move left, and the robot at index 2 will move right. After 3 seconds, the positions are [-3,-1,1]. The distance between the robot at index 0 and 1 is abs(-3 - (-1)) = 2. The distance between the robot at index 0 and 2 is abs(-3 - 1) = 4. The distance between the robot at index 1 and 2 is abs(-1 - 1) = 2. The sum of the pairs of all distances = 2 + 4 + 2 = 8. </pre> <p><strong class="example">Example 2:</strong></p> <pre> <strong>Input:</strong> nums = [1,0], s = &quot;RL&quot;, d = 2 <strong>Output:</strong> 5 <strong>Explanation:</strong> After 1 second, the positions are [2,-1]. After 2 seconds, the positions are [3,-2]. The distance between the two robots is abs(-2 - 3) = 5. </pre> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p><strong>Constraints:</strong></p> <ul> <li><code>2 &lt;= nums.length &lt;= 10<sup>5</sup></code></li> <li><code>-2 * 10<sup>9</sup>&nbsp;&lt;= nums[i] &lt;= 2 * 10<sup>9</sup></code></li> <li><code>0 &lt;= d &lt;= 10<sup>9</sup></code></li> <li><code>nums.length == s.length&nbsp;</code></li> <li><code>s</code> consists of &#39;L&#39; and &#39;R&#39; only</li> <li><code>nums[i]</code>&nbsp;will be unique.</li> </ul> # Solution # Intuition
**The main intuition is when 2 robots collide, it switches direction. but for the purpose of finding the pair wise distance, it is equivalent to not switching the robots' position and keep the same direction.**
<!-- Describe your first thoughts on how to solve this problem. -->

# Approach
To solve this we need to keep in mind these 2 things -

- Here a new vector is used of type `<long long>` because when we add or sub the distance after time `d`, then it may cause an overflow.
- To calculate the pair wise distance sum we need to understand how prefix sum can be used:

 Imagine finding distance of a4 with all elements before it
 `(a4-a1)+(a4-a2)+(a4-a3) -> (a4+a4+a4)-(a1+a2+3) -> (i*a4) - prefixsum`
 So this formula can be used only if the numbers are sorted.

**IF YOU LIKED THE EXPLANATION PLEASE DO UPVOTE IT**

Here's a detailed explanation of the code:

1. `int mod = 1e9 + 7;`: This line defines a constant integer `mod` with a value of 1e9 + 7. This value is used for modulo operations later in the code.

2. `vector<long long> newNums;`: A new vector of type `long long` named `newNums` is declared. This vector will store the modified values of the input vector `nums`. Using `long long` ensures that large integers can be handled without causing overflow.

3. The `for` loop iterates through the elements of the `nums` vector and copies each element to the `newNums` vector. This creates a deep copy of the input vector.

4. The next `for` loop iterates through the `newNums` vector. For each element at index `i`, it checks the corresponding character in the string `s`. If `s[i]` is 'L', it subtracts the value of `d` from `newNums[i`. If `s[i]` is anything other than 'L' (i.e., 'R'), it adds the value of `d` to `newNums[i]`.

5. After these modifications, the `newNums` vector contains the elements of `nums` adjusted according to the operations specified in the string `s`.

6. `sort(newNums.begin(), newNums.end());`: The `newNums` vector is sorted in ascending order.

7. Two variables `distance` and `prefixSum` are declared and initialized to 0. These variables will be used to calculate the sum of distances between elements in the sorted `newNums` vector.

8. Another `for` loop iterates through the elements of the sorted `newNums` vector. For each element at index `i`, it calculates the distance using the formula:
 ```
 distance = (distance + (1LL * i * newNums[i] - prefixSum)) % mod;
 ```
 - `1LL * i * newNums[i]` calculates the contribution of the current element to the distance.
 - `prefixSum` is used to keep track of the cumulative sum of elements in the sorted vector.
 - The result is updated in the `distance` variable, and it is calculated modulo `mod` to ensure the result remains within reasonable bounds.

9. Finally, the function returns the calculated `distance`, which represents the sum of distances between the modified elements in the sorted vector, taking into account the operations specified in the string `s` and applying modulo `mod`.

This code efficiently calculates the desired sum of distances while handling potential integer overflow by using `long long` and modulo operations.
<!-- Describe your approach to solving the problem. -->

# Complexity
- Time complexity: O(nlogn)
<!-- Add your time complexity here, e.g. $$O(n)$$ -->

- Space complexity: O(n)
<!-- Add your space complexity here, e.g. $$O(n)$$ -->

# Code
```
class Solution {
 int mod = 1e9 + 7 ;
public:
 int sumDistance(vector<int>& nums, string s, int d) {
 vector<long long> newNums ; // Create a new vector to store modified values
 for(int i = 0; i<nums.size(); i++)
 newNums.push_back(nums[i]) ;

 for(int i = 0; i<newNums.size(); i++) {
 if(s[i] == 'L')
// -2000000000 - 1000000000 can cause overflow if <long long> type not used
 newNums[i] = newNums[i] - d ;
 else
// 2000000000 + 1000000000 can cause overflow if <long long> type not used
 newNums[i] = newNums[i] + d ;
 }

 sort(newNums.begin(), newNums.end()) ;
 long long distance = 0, prefixSum = 0 ;
 for(int i = 0; i<newNums.size(); i++) {
// The formula calculates the distance and updates 'prefixSum' for the next iteration.
 distance = (distance + (1LL*i*newNums[i] - prefixSum)) % mod ;
 prefixSum = (prefixSum + newNums[i]) % mod ;
 }
 return distance ;
 }
};
```
